/* Anchor Notes — Operations & AI implementation editions (Apr–Nov 2025). */
(() => {
  const source = (label, url) => ({ label, url });
  const edition = (data) => ({
    hero: 'IMG/anchor-studio-forum.png',
    ...data,
    sources: data.sources || [
      source('NIST AI Risk Management Framework', 'https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework'),
      source('NIST Generative AI Profile', 'https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf')
    ]
  });

  window.AnchorNoteIssues = Object.assign(window.AnchorNoteIssues || {}, {
    'modern-concierge': edition({
      date: 'Nov 2025', tags: 'AI Agents · Web Design',
      title: 'The Modern Client Concierge',
      dek: 'A premium digital welcome does not impersonate a person. It notices intent, asks fewer and better questions, and carries a qualified conversation to the right human without losing the thread.',
      sections: [
        { heading: 'Hospitality is a systems discipline', body: `A website is often asked to do two contradictory things: create a sense of occasion and collect enough information to move a commercial conversation forward. Luxury service resolves that tension by making the first exchange feel considered rather than extractive. A modern concierge begins with the visitor’s context—what they are trying to decide, what they have already seen, and whether they need orientation, proof, or a path to action. It does not open with a maze of form fields or a theatrical claim that it is human. It offers a clear invitation, explains its role, and earns the next question. The distinction matters. A visitor who feels understood will volunteer useful detail; a visitor who feels processed will leave or provide the minimum. The interface, copy, and handoff workflow must therefore be designed together. The agent is not a decoration on the site. It is a steward of a first impression, and its manners become part of the brand.` },
        { heading: 'Design the conversation around a real decision', body: `The best intake conversations are built backward from the decision a team needs to make. For an agency, that may be whether the request is a brand engagement, a new website, an AI implementation, or a short advisory conversation; for a professional service firm, it may be urgency, fit, budget range, geography, or the need for regulated expertise. Translate those distinctions into a short, progressive dialogue. Begin broad enough that visitors can describe the work in their own language. Then ask only the questions that change the recommended path. A well-designed concierge can summarize the brief in the visitor’s words, surface relevant work, and invite a calendar action when readiness is clear. It should never manufacture urgency, speculate about pricing, or offer commitments the delivery team has not approved. In practice, the conversation is a compact brief-writing exercise: goal, audience, constraints, timing, evidence, and desired next step. That produces a more useful lead than a longer form because it preserves the why behind the fields.` },
        { heading: 'Knowledge must be curated before it is conversational', body: `An agent can only represent what the organization is willing to stand behind. Before deployment, assemble a deliberately small knowledge set: current capabilities, approved case studies, service boundaries, basic process language, response-time expectations, and escalation contacts. Remove stale decks, unverified performance claims, private client material, and contradictory pricing language. Each item should have an owner and review date. This is less glamorous than selecting a model, but it is where credibility is won. When a visitor asks an unfamiliar question, the appropriate response is not an elegant guess; it is a graceful acknowledgement and a route to a person. Source-aware answers are particularly important when a concierge recommends a service or references a client outcome. The organization should be able to trace the claim, correct it, and learn from the question. That discipline turns the agent into a dependable front door rather than an improvisational sales script.` },
        { heading: 'Make the human handoff feel like continuation', body: `A qualified conversation should arrive with a human colleague as continuity, not a reset. The handoff record needs the visitor’s objective, the questions asked, the answers supplied, materials shared, permission preferences, and the agent’s recommended next step. The colleague should be able to read it in under a minute and respond without asking the visitor to repeat themselves. Establish service levels for different signals: a high-intent project inquiry may merit rapid personal outreach; a research-stage visitor may receive a useful resource and a light invitation to return. Consent belongs in this design. Visitors should understand when their information is being saved, how follow-up works, and how to opt out. The client relationship begins before a contract, so the same dignity expected in delivery should appear in this transition. A concise, well-written handoff is often more valuable than a higher volume of unqualified leads.` },
        { heading: 'Measure trust as well as conversion', body: `Conversion rate is necessary but incomplete. Review the ratio of conversations that reach a useful next step, the accuracy of routing, time to human response, repeat questions, abandonment points, calendar attendance, and the quality of opportunities accepted by the team. Read a small sample of transcripts every week. Look for signs that the concierge is overexplaining, asking questions in the wrong order, or creating false confidence. The strongest metric may be qualitative: does the sales or client-services team feel better prepared for the first conversation? If the answer is no, the system is producing activity without improving the experience. Tune one variable at a time—welcome language, knowledge coverage, a question, a routing rule—and preserve a record of what changed. A concierge becomes excellent through editorial maintenance and operational attention, not through a one-time launch.` },
        { heading: 'A concierge charter', body: `Write a short charter before launch. Name the jobs the agent may do, the decisions it may not make, the claims it may use, the data it may collect, and the person responsible for review. Include examples of a helpful answer, an uncertain answer, and an escalation. Train the team on how to use the handoff record and where to report errors. Then pilot the experience on a single high-value journey rather than across every page. This gives the business a chance to hear the conversation as a customer would. The aim is not to remove people from the welcome. It is to reserve their attention for the moment it can make the greatest difference. A modern client concierge is successful when visitors feel guided, colleagues feel prepared, and the business can explain exactly how the system serves both.` }
      ],
      sources: [source('NIST AI Risk Management Framework', 'https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework'), source('FTC: Keep your AI claims in check', 'https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/blog/2023/02/keep-your-ai-claims-check')]
    }),
    'responsible-ai-guide': edition({
      date: 'Oct 2025', tags: 'Responsible AI · Governance', title: 'The Executive Guide to Responsible AI',
      dek: 'Responsible AI is not a policy binder added after procurement. It is a practical way to decide where intelligence belongs, what evidence it may use, and who remains accountable when the stakes rise.',
      sections: [
        { heading: 'Governance begins with a business choice', body: `Boards and executive teams do not need to become model engineers to govern AI well. They need a reliable way to distinguish experiments from operating decisions. Start with an inventory of where AI is already appearing: purchased software, employee-led experimentation, marketing tools, customer-service assistants, analytics products, and internal knowledge retrieval. For each use, document the business purpose, affected people, data involved, decision consequence, owner, and current safeguard. This creates a map more useful than a generic statement of principles. It reveals where a simple productivity assistant has quietly become a system influencing pricing, eligibility, employment, or customer treatment. Governance should follow consequence. A low-risk drafting tool and a high-impact recommendation engine deserve different review, evidence, and escalation requirements. The executive responsibility is to make those distinctions explicit, fund the controls that matter, and ensure the organization can explain its choices in plain language.` },
        { heading: 'Use risk tiers that change behavior', body: `A risk tier is valuable only if it produces a different operating requirement. A practical approach separates tools that create internal drafts from systems that affect customers, make recommendations about people, handle sensitive information, or trigger irreversible action. The first group may need approved-use guidance and training. The next may require source restrictions, testing, monitoring, disclosure, and named review. The most consequential systems should have formal approval, documented impact assessment, incident response, and human authority to pause the service. Do not classify by technology fashion; classify by the harm a wrong output could cause and by how difficult that harm is to reverse. A model can be sophisticated and low risk in one setting, while a familiar automation can be high risk in another. This framing gives leaders a shared language for investment: stronger safeguards are not bureaucracy, but proportional care for more consequential work.` },
        { heading: 'Privacy is a design constraint, not a footnote', body: `Teams often discover data questions too late because they treat the prompt box as a private workspace. It is not. Every implementation should establish what information may be entered, retained, transmitted, used for evaluation, or connected to other systems. Separate public, internal, confidential, and regulated material. Give people an easy way to recognize those categories in the flow of work. Contracts and vendor settings deserve review, but so does the interface: a clear boundary at the moment of use prevents more mistakes than a policy few people remember. Minimize data by default and avoid collecting fields simply because a form can ask for them. Where a service does retain a conversation or personal information, say so plainly. Respectful disclosure is both risk control and brand practice; it tells customers that the business sees their information as entrusted, not merely available.` },
        { heading: 'Evaluation should resemble the real world', body: `A polished demonstration cannot prove a system is ready. Build an evaluation set from realistic work: straightforward requests, ambiguous cases, incomplete information, attempts to override instructions, sensitive content, and situations where the correct action is to abstain. Define what good looks like before testing—accuracy, groundedness, tone, accessibility, timeliness, and appropriate escalation. Then review outputs with the people who understand the work, not only the people who built the interface. Keep a record of failures and the changes made in response. This is especially important when teams modify prompts, add source material, or change vendors; each alteration can affect behavior. Evaluations should be lightweight enough to repeat and serious enough to catch the failure that would matter to a client. Reliability is not a permanent property of a system. It is a habit of testing against the conditions in which it will actually be used.` },
        { heading: 'Prepare for the moment something goes wrong', body: `An incident plan need not assume catastrophe to be useful. Identify how a customer, employee, or partner can report a harmful or inaccurate interaction; who receives that report; how the relevant record is preserved; who can stop the system; how affected people are informed; and how the lesson returns to the design. Rehearse a small scenario. Imagine an assistant exposes a stale policy, routes a sensitive request incorrectly, or produces a discriminatory recommendation. The exercise often reveals missing ownership faster than a committee meeting. It also reinforces a healthy expectation: every system has limits, and a responsible organization is judged by how quickly and honestly it responds when those limits appear. Leaders should ask for evidence of readiness, not a promise that failure is impossible.` },
        { heading: 'Make accountability visible', body: `Responsible AI becomes credible when employees and customers can see its operating values in action. Publish concise internal rules, train by role, provide examples, and create a non-punitive channel for questions. For customer-facing uses, disclose automated assistance in language people can understand and offer a human route. Maintain an owner register rather than a vague cross-functional mandate. Review the program on a regular cadence: new use cases, data changes, incidents, metrics, and outstanding decisions. This approach avoids two common errors—freezing useful experimentation until a perfect policy exists, or permitting experimentation without any durable responsibility. The executive aim is not to predict every technological change. It is to build an organization capable of noticing risk early, making proportional decisions, and preserving trust while it learns.` }
      ], sources: [source('NIST AI RMF', 'https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework'), source('NIST Generative AI Profile', 'https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf'), source('OECD AI Principles', 'https://oecd.ai/en/ai-principles')]
    }),
    'pilots-that-learn': edition({
      date: 'Sep 2025', tags: 'AI Implementation · Change Management', title: 'Why AI Pilots Stall—and How to Design One That Learns',
      dek: 'Most pilots do not fail because the model is weak. They stall because the team never agreed on the decision, the owner, the baseline, or the evidence that would justify moving forward.',
      sections: [
        { heading: 'A pilot is a question with a deadline', body: `The word pilot can hide a great deal of indecision. Teams use it to mean a demo, an internal sandbox, a vendor trial, or a nearly complete product. A learning pilot is more precise: it is a time-bounded test of a business hypothesis under controlled conditions. Its brief should state the journey being improved, the people affected, the current baseline, the intended outcome, the risks that must not increase, and the decision that will be made when the test ends. That decision may be scale, revise, pause, or stop. Without it, teams accumulate interesting transcripts and no authority to act on them. The discipline of a clear question also protects against oversized scope. Instead of asking whether AI can transform customer service, ask whether a source-grounded assistant can reduce the time a named team spends locating approved answers while preserving escalation quality. That is a question an organization can genuinely learn from.` },
        { heading: 'Choose work with enough repetition and consequence', body: `A good first use case sits where repetition meets meaningful value. It occurs often enough to observe, has an identifiable owner, and creates a real cost in delay, inconsistency, or avoidable searching. It is not so entangled with every system in the business that learning requires a transformation program. Teams are often tempted by the most visible or imaginative possibility; a better pilot may be the unglamorous preparation step that gives people back time each week. Map the existing work honestly, including exceptions and workarounds. If there is no stable process, the pilot may be exposing a workflow problem rather than an AI opportunity. That is still useful learning, but it calls for process design before automation. Start with the part of the work the team can describe, measure, and safely change.` },
        { heading: 'Build the baseline before the excitement', body: `A pilot cannot demonstrate value if nobody knows the current condition. Capture a modest baseline: cycle time, rework, completion quality, volume, handoff delay, customer response, and the amount of expert attention consumed. Add a small qualitative record of what makes the work difficult. Avoid the false precision of a giant dashboard; the point is comparison, not theater. Then decide which outcomes must remain protected. A faster draft process that increases factual corrections, or a shorter intake flow that produces poorer client fit, is not a win. Baselines also help leadership hear the project in operational terms. Rather than promising that a tool is innovative, the team can say which friction it is attempting to remove and what evidence would count as improvement.` },
        { heading: 'Invite the people who will live with the result', body: `Adoption is not a communications phase at the end of a pilot. It begins when frontline colleagues help shape the work. Ask them where a system would be genuinely helpful, what language it must understand, which errors would be unacceptable, and what would make them ignore it. Include skeptics; they often identify the exception that breaks an elegant diagram. Give participants a way to correct outputs, report failures, and see that their feedback changed something. Training should use their actual cases, not generic sample prompts. The goal is not enthusiasm on command. It is informed participation. People support a change when they can see how it improves a specific part of their work and when they retain the authority to challenge it.` },
        { heading: 'Treat the pilot as an operating rehearsal', body: `Even a limited test needs real operating conditions: approved source material, access controls, disclosure decisions, a support route, and someone empowered to pause the experience. Test edge cases deliberately. What happens when the source is silent, a request is sensitive, the system is uncertain, or a customer needs a person? The answers reveal whether the pilot is ready for real use. Keep a decision log that records changes to instructions, knowledge, and routing. This makes the learning portable when the test moves to a new team or vendor. It also prevents a common failure mode: no one can explain why a system improved or deteriorated after several unrecorded adjustments.` },
        { heading: 'Close the loop with a decision', body: `At the end of the defined period, bring evidence to the same leaders and practitioners who approved the brief. Compare the baseline and results, inspect a representative sample, summarize feedback, name the unresolved risks, and make the next decision. Scaling should include a plan for ownership, training, measurement, and maintenance—not merely more licenses. Revising should identify the one or two changes worth testing next. Stopping is a legitimate result when the use case lacks fit or the safeguards are too costly for the value. A well-run pilot creates confidence even when it does not proceed, because it replaces speculation with a visible, responsible decision. That is the deeper purpose: build organizational muscle for learning, not a collection of unfinished experiments.` }
      ], sources: [source('NIST AI RMF Playbook', 'https://airc.nist.gov/AI_RMF_Knowledge_Base/Playbook'), source('OpenAI: A practical guide to building agents', 'https://cdn.openai.com/business-guides-and-resources/a-practical-guide-to-building-agents.pdf')]
    }),
    'buy-configure-build': edition({
      date: 'Aug 2025', tags: 'AI Tools Development · Procurement', title: 'The AI Tool Stack: Buy, Configure, or Build?',
      dek: 'The right question is not which AI platform looks most capable in a demonstration. It is where the work is common, where the business is distinctive, and where integration or control creates lasting value.',
      sections: [
        { heading: 'Procurement begins with the work, not the catalogue', body: `Tool selection becomes expensive when an organization starts with features rather than the job to be done. Begin with a map of the workflow: trigger, inputs, decisions, handoffs, exceptions, outputs, and the consequences of a mistake. Then identify what is genuinely common across the market and what expresses the organization’s differentiating expertise. Commodity work—meeting transcription, basic drafting, general search, routine scheduling—often benefits from a reputable product that can be configured responsibly. Distinctive work—specialized judgment, proprietary knowledge, a signature client experience, or a critical integration—may justify a tailored layer. The purpose of this analysis is not to make every system custom. It is to avoid building what is already a service and avoid forcing a generic product to carry the work that makes the business valuable.` },
        { heading: 'Buy when the problem is stable and ordinary', body: `A purchased tool is often the strongest choice when the need is widely understood, the provider has mature security and support practices, and the business can accept the product’s operating model. Buying can reduce maintenance, accelerate adoption, and let a team focus on process rather than infrastructure. But procurement should still test the actual experience: permissions, retention settings, exportability, accessibility, administrative controls, integration quality, and the provider’s change practices. A feature list cannot answer whether a team can govern the tool once it is embedded in daily work. Select products with a clear owner inside the business and a plan for training, support, and offboarding. The cheapest subscription is not necessarily the lowest-cost choice if it creates fragmented knowledge, manual workarounds, or a new dependency nobody can manage.` },
        { heading: 'Configure when the value lies in the operating model', body: `Many valuable AI systems are not built from zero; they are thoughtfully configured around a specific workflow. Configuration may include approved knowledge sources, carefully designed prompts, role-based access, structured intake, routing rules, templates, and human review. This is where service design becomes technology design. A generic model can become a useful proposal assistant, client concierge, or internal knowledge guide when it is given context and boundaries the organization owns. The work deserves the same craft as a custom application: write acceptance criteria, test representative cases, keep instructions legible, and name who maintains the content. Configuration is not a shortcut around governance. It is a way to concentrate investment on the behavior that matters without taking on unnecessary platform engineering.` },
        { heading: 'Build when differentiation or control is material', body: `Custom development earns its cost when a system must connect proprietary data, orchestrate several business rules, create an experience competitors cannot buy, or operate under requirements that off-the-shelf products cannot meet. The case should be made in operational terms. What outcome cannot be achieved through process and configuration? Which integration is essential? What ownership and maintenance capacity will exist after launch? A custom interface on top of a third-party model still needs monitoring, evaluation, access control, observability, and a plan for provider changes. Building is not synonymous with owning every layer; it is a decision to own the experience, rules, and integration that are strategically important. The strongest custom systems are narrow, measurable, and designed to evolve.` },
        { heading: 'Avoid the false binary', body: `Most capable stacks are hybrid. A team may buy a secure transcription service, configure a knowledge assistant, and build a lightweight client portal that brings approved insights into a distinct experience. The architecture should make these boundaries deliberate. Keep portable content and business rules separate from vendor-specific configuration where possible. Document integrations, data flows, and fallbacks. This reduces lock-in without pretending that change has no cost. It also allows the organization to replace one layer without dismantling the entire operation. Executives should ask for a roadmap that names which components are commodity, which are configured, which are custom, and why. That single view makes spend, risk, and maintenance easier to govern.` },
        { heading: 'Decide with a total-cost lens', body: `Licenses and development estimates are only the opening figures. Include implementation, data preparation, security review, training, change management, administration, support, evaluation, and periodic redesign. Estimate the cost of delay and the cost of a poor fit. Then compare those investments to a defined baseline rather than an abstract promise of productivity. A modest configured solution that becomes part of daily work may create more value than an ambitious custom platform that never receives stewardship. The final decision should be reversible where possible, with clear exit terms and ownership of essential data. Good procurement protects optionality while giving the team enough stability to learn. That balance—practical today, adaptable tomorrow—is the hallmark of an AI stack built to last.` }
      ], sources: [source('NIST AI RMF', 'https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework'), source('CISA: Secure by Design', 'https://www.cisa.gov/securebydesign')]
    }),
    'brand-voice-asset': edition({
      date: 'Jul 2025', tags: 'Brand Strategy · Knowledge Systems', title: 'Your Brand Voice Is an Operating Asset',
      dek: 'A voice guide is more than a creative reference. When it is translated into usable examples, source material, and review practices, it becomes the operating context that keeps AI-assisted work recognizably yours.',
      sections: [
        { heading: 'Voice lives in decisions, not adjectives', body: `Most brand guides contain adjectives—confident, warm, elevated, direct—that become too vague the moment a writer or system must use them. A real voice is visible in recurring choices: what the organization leads with, what it refuses to exaggerate, how it addresses uncertainty, which claims require evidence, how it makes a request, and where it prefers a shorter sentence to a flourish. To make voice operational, collect approved examples from the work itself: a strong homepage paragraph, a thoughtful client email, a concise proposal, an effective error message, and a note that handled a difficult moment with grace. Annotate why each works. This turns taste into a teachable pattern. An AI system cannot protect a brand from a list of adjectives; it can work with clear examples, boundaries, and a named editor who can improve them.` },
        { heading: 'Treat the voice system as a knowledge product', body: `A usable voice system has components. It names the audience and the relationship the business is building. It identifies message pillars, terms to prefer or avoid, claims that need substantiation, reading level, accessibility considerations, and approved calls to action. It contains example transformations: a flat statement made more specific, a hype-filled sentence made more credible, a technical explanation made clear without becoming simplistic. It also documents what must never appear—unsupported superlatives, insensitive language, false urgency, confidential client detail, or promises the organization cannot keep. Store these elements where people work, not in a presentation that disappears after kickoff. Version them, assign an owner, and review them when the business changes. The result is a shared editorial asset that benefits people before it benefits any model.` },
        { heading: 'Context must be selective to be useful', body: `Feeding every historical document into a system is not a voice strategy. It creates noise, carries forward outdated positioning, and makes it difficult to understand what influenced an output. Curate a smaller approved set and tag it by purpose: service explanation, point of view, case study, conversion copy, internal communication, or customer support. Pair those examples with a current brief and explicit instructions about audience, format, evidence, and desired action. A model should receive enough context to make a disciplined draft, not a warehouse of material it cannot distinguish. The editorial team’s job is to decide which source represents the present standard. That curation is a form of brand protection and a major source of efficiency.` },
        { heading: 'Drafting needs review routes, not blind trust', body: `AI-assisted content should enter a defined editorial flow. The system may propose a structure, summarize approved material, produce variants, or identify gaps, while a writer remains accountable for meaning, accuracy, rhythm, and fit. Establish a review level based on consequence. A low-stakes internal outline may need a quick scan; a public claim about client outcomes, pricing, regulated services, or performance requires source verification and appropriate approval. The review should assess more than grammar. Ask: is the point of view clear, is the claim supportable, does the copy sound like this organization, and is the next action respectful? These questions preserve the human signature that makes a brand worth remembering.` },
        { heading: 'Measure coherence over volume', body: `Content systems are often measured by how much they can produce. A better measure is whether customers encounter a consistent and useful point of view across the moments that matter. Audit a small sample of pages, emails, proposals, social posts, and agent conversations. Look for unsupported claims, sudden shifts in formality, duplicate language, inaccessible jargon, or calls to action that do not match the visitor’s stage. Track revision patterns: if editors repeatedly fix the same issue, improve the context or the source material instead of relying on heroic cleanup. Coherence compounds. It lowers review effort, makes teams more confident, and helps customers recognize the business without being shouted at.` },
        { heading: 'Build a living editorial council', body: `Give the system a modest but real stewardship cadence. A small group representing brand, delivery, legal or risk where relevant, and customer-facing teams can review new examples, retire stale language, and resolve contested decisions. Publish the updates in a form writers and operators can use. Invite questions from the people creating the work; their friction is often the clearest signal that a rule is missing. This is not a committee for policing every sentence. It is a way to keep the organization’s language connected to its actual standards. When brand voice is treated as an operating asset, AI can help teams move faster without sanding away the judgment, dignity, and specificity that make their work distinct.` }
      ], sources: [source('W3C Plain Language resources', 'https://www.w3.org/WAI/tips/writing/'), source('W3C Web Content Accessibility Guidelines', 'https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/wcag/')]
    }),
    'education-program': edition({
      date: 'Jun 2025', tags: 'AI Education · Corporate Training', title: 'Designing an AI Education Program People Will Actually Use',
      dek: 'Attendance is not adoption. A useful education program gives each role a practical job to improve, examples that resemble real work, and enough governance to use new tools with confidence.',
      sections: [
        { heading: 'Start with work people recognize', body: `Training fails when it introduces technology as an abstract future rather than a response to today’s friction. Before designing a curriculum, speak with people in the roles the program is meant to serve. Ask where they lose time, what they repeatedly rewrite, which information is hard to find, where decisions slow down, and what they worry could go wrong. Convert those answers into role-specific scenarios. A project manager may need to turn a messy meeting into accountable next steps; a marketer may need to develop a sourced brief; a client-services professional may need to prepare a thoughtful response without exposing private information. These are better learning units than a generic catalog of prompt tricks because participants can test the lesson against work they will perform tomorrow.` },
        { heading: 'Teach judgment alongside technique', body: `Prompt engineering is useful when it is taught as clear briefing: define the objective, provide relevant context, set constraints, request a format, establish acceptance criteria, and review the result. But technique without judgment creates a different risk—people become efficient at producing plausible material they have not evaluated. Every session should therefore include verification, privacy, copyright and attribution awareness where relevant, disclosure, and escalation. Show an output that is fluent but wrong. Show a request that should not be entered. Show how to say “I do not know” or bring in a colleague. Participants leave more capable when they understand both the opportunity and the boundary. This is especially important for leaders, who set the pace through the questions they ask and the examples they reward.` },
        { heading: 'Use a ladder, not a single workshop', body: `One presentation can build awareness; it rarely changes behavior. Create a learning ladder. Begin with a concise orientation that explains approved tools, essential guardrails, and the organization’s point of view. Follow it with role studios using real scenarios, then office hours where people can bring unfinished work and receive coaching. Offer a small advanced track for champions who will help maintain prompt libraries, evaluation examples, or local workflows. Record short demonstrations for refreshers, but do not mistake a video library for a program. Learning requires practice, feedback, and a social permission to ask basic questions. A predictable rhythm is more valuable than an occasional inspirational event.` },
        { heading: 'Create artifacts that survive the session', body: `Each learning experience should produce something usable: a role-specific prompt pattern, a checklist for reviewing a draft, a before-and-after example, a glossary, or a short decision tree for choosing the right tool. Store these artifacts in a place people can find at the moment of work. Keep the language concise and identify who owns updates. The best prompt library is not an archive of clever one-liners; it is a collection of repeatable instructions tied to defined tasks, approved sources, expected outputs, and cautions. It should include examples of when not to use the prompt. That completeness makes learning safer and reduces the tendency for every individual to invent a private, inconsistent method.` },
        { heading: 'Measure transfer, not applause', body: `Satisfaction scores can reveal whether a session was clear and respectful, but they cannot show whether behavior changed. Measure participation by role, repeat use of approved workflows, confidence before and after practice, quality of submitted work, questions received at office hours, and the time or rework avoided in a selected process. Listen for uneven adoption: if only enthusiasts are using the tools, the program may be missing the people whose work would benefit most. Review real examples with managers and participants. The evidence should guide curriculum revisions. If people understand a policy but struggle to apply it, add scenarios; if they produce drafts quickly but review them poorly, focus on evaluation. Training becomes credible when it changes the work, not merely the calendar.` },
        { heading: 'Make leadership participation visible', body: `Leaders need their own learning path. They should understand enough to sponsor sensible use cases, ask for evidence rather than hype, and model responsible behavior in their own communication. A leader who uses an unapproved tool or treats AI output as final signals that the guardrails are optional. Conversely, a leader who shares a careful use case, names the review performed, and credits the team for surfacing a risk makes adoption safer. Education is cultural infrastructure. When the program respects participants’ expertise, protects their time, and connects new capability to meaningful work, people do not have to be persuaded to use it—they can see why it belongs.` }
      ], sources: [source('NIST AI RMF Playbook', 'https://airc.nist.gov/AI_RMF_Knowledge_Base/Playbook'), source('W3C Accessibility Fundamentals', 'https://www.w3.org/WAI/fundamentals/accessibility-intro/')]
    }),
    'boardroom-value': edition({
      date: 'May 2025', tags: 'AI Strategy · Governance', title: 'The Boardroom Guide to AI Value',
      dek: 'AI value is not a promise of universal efficiency. It is a portfolio of measurable improvements in time, quality, resilience, customer experience, and learning—balanced against the risk and stewardship required.',
      sections: [
        { heading: 'Move from capability theater to an investment thesis', body: `Board conversations often begin with a question that is too broad: what is our AI strategy? The useful question is narrower: where can intelligence improve a strategic outcome, what must remain true for the organization, and what evidence would justify continued investment? An investment thesis ties a specific use case to business value. It names the baseline, the affected stakeholders, the owner, the expected mechanism of improvement, the costs of implementation and maintenance, and the risks that cannot be traded away. This turns an abstract technology discussion into a portfolio decision. Some initiatives will create immediate operational relief; others will build knowledge, experience, or resilience that compounds over time. Both can be valid, but they should not be evaluated with the same clock or metric.` },
        { heading: 'Use a balanced scorecard', body: `A credible scorecard avoids the temptation to reduce AI to labor savings. Track time to a useful outcome, output quality, error or exception rates, customer response, employee confidence, cost to serve, compliance or risk signals, and the ability to learn from the system. Not every use case needs every measure, but every use case needs more than adoption. High usage may indicate genuine value or simply a mandated new process; low usage may reveal poor fit, lack of training, or an experience that does not earn trust. Pair quantitative signals with sampled outputs and frontline feedback. Boards should expect management to explain what the numbers mean in the context of the work, not merely report an activity count.` },
        { heading: 'Separate value horizons', body: `A portfolio benefits from distinct horizons. Near-term opportunities reduce a clear friction in an existing workflow. Medium-term work connects knowledge, systems, or teams in a way that improves service or decision quality. Longer-term bets may explore a new product, operating model, or relationship with customers. Mixing these horizons creates disappointment: an exploratory capability is judged as if it should pay back immediately, while a mature workflow is allowed to continue without evidence. Set different decision gates for each. A short operational pilot might require a measurable improvement within weeks; a new capability may require staged learning, governance, and market validation. Clarity about horizon helps the organization remain ambitious without becoming careless.` },
        { heading: 'Value depends on adoption and operating capacity', body: `A model does not deliver value by itself. Someone must curate sources, train users, monitor quality, resolve exceptions, review vendors, and maintain the workflow as policies and customer expectations change. These are operating costs, but they are also the work that protects the investment. Boards should ask whether management has named these responsibilities and funded them. A business can buy a promising platform and still lose value through incomplete data, unclear ownership, or a lack of change support. The operating model belongs in the business case from the start. It is the difference between a tool that appears in a slide deck and a capability that changes how work is reliably done.` },
        { heading: 'Require evidence of responsible scaling', body: `Scaling should not mean deploying the same experience everywhere because it worked somewhere. Require evidence that the underlying workflow, data quality, risk tier, and user needs are sufficiently similar. Ask what safeguards will change as volume or consequence increases. Review incident patterns, complaints, evaluation results, and the quality of human handoffs. Make it possible to pause or narrow a system if the evidence changes. This is not a sign of weak conviction; it is a sign that leadership understands the difference between a controlled use case and an enterprise operating capability. Responsible scale preserves the conditions that made the initial result credible.` },
        { heading: 'Govern with curiosity and discipline', body: `The best board questions are both ambitious and exacting: Which customer or employee outcome are we improving? What are we learning that we could not learn otherwise? What decision will this evidence enable? Where could the system fail, and who owns the response? What makes this capability defensible? These questions encourage management to connect technology to strategy while keeping accountability visible. They also give teams permission to stop weak initiatives and strengthen promising ones. The aim is not to look advanced. It is to allocate capital, attention, and trust with the same care applied to any meaningful business system.` }
      ], sources: [source('NIST AI Risk Management Framework', 'https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework'), source('OECD AI Policy Observatory', 'https://oecd.ai/')]
    }),
    'lead-qualification': edition({
      date: 'Apr 2025', tags: 'AI Agents · Lead Generation', title: 'From Inquiry to Intelligence: Reimagining Lead Qualification',
      dek: 'Lead qualification should feel like a useful first consultation, not a gate. The job is to understand the work, protect the visitor’s time, and bring the right human into a better-prepared conversation.',
      sections: [
        { heading: 'Qualification is a service moment', body: `Traditional lead forms optimize for collection: name, email, company, budget, timeline. Those fields may be useful, but they often ask a visitor to translate an unfinished problem into a sales taxonomy before the business has offered any value. A stronger approach begins with assistance. Let the visitor describe the ambition in ordinary language, then use a concise conversation to clarify the decision, the audience, the current obstacle, and the desired next step. This produces better context for both sides. It also respects that many high-value inquiries begin before a client knows which service category they need. The goal is not to qualify people out. It is to make an intelligent routing decision while giving everyone who arrives a dignified, useful interaction.` },
        { heading: 'Ask only questions that change the path', body: `Every question has a cost. Before adding one, identify the decision it changes. Does timing determine whether an inquiry needs a rapid response? Does organization size affect the appropriate engagement? Does a budget range help frame options, or is it being used as a blunt filter? A well-designed flow asks broad questions first, then follows the answers. A visitor interested in an AI education program needs different discovery than someone seeking a complete brand and web engagement. Progressive disclosure keeps the exchange shorter and more natural. It also reduces the temptation to build a massive form that nobody completes honestly. If a question does not alter the recommendation, handoff, or next action, it probably belongs later—or not at all.` },
        { heading: 'Use intelligence to prepare, not to decide alone', body: `An AI assistant can summarize a conversation, identify likely service fit, suggest relevant work, and draft a handoff note. It should not make final judgments about the value of a person, the seriousness of their need, or the commercial terms available to them. Keep human authority over eligibility, pricing, contracts, and sensitive situations. Design routing rules that are transparent to the internal team and easy to review. If the system is uncertain, it should offer a general resource, ask a clarifying question, or escalate—not force a classification. This protects prospects from arbitrary treatment and protects the business from losing the unusual opportunity that does not resemble a standard lead.` },
        { heading: 'Connect capture to a respectful follow-up system', body: `Information has value only when it moves to an accountable next step. Define where inquiry data goes, who receives it, what context is included, how quickly a person responds, and what happens when no follow-up is appropriate. Avoid copying sensitive conversation text into systems that do not need it. Capture consent preferences clearly, and honor them. The first human response should demonstrate continuity: acknowledge the project in the visitor’s own terms, reference what they shared, and offer a relevant next move. That might be a short diagnostic call, a concise resource, a portfolio example, or a polite explanation of fit. A generic autoresponder can undo the care of an otherwise thoughtful intake experience.` },
        { heading: 'Score the workflow, not the person', body: `Lead scoring can be useful when it helps a team prioritize response, but it should be designed as an operational aid rather than a hidden judgment about worth. Score signals that relate to service delivery—clear timing, stated need, existing relationship, geographic constraints where relevant, or a requested capability—then audit the outcomes. Are good opportunities being missed? Are certain groups being routed differently without a sound business reason? Is the score encouraging staff to ignore complex but promising inquiries? Keep the criteria simple enough to explain and change. Qualitative review remains essential because a commercial relationship often begins with nuance no score can capture.` },
        { heading: 'Learn from the conversations you decline', body: `Closed or redirected inquiries are valuable evidence. Review why they were not a fit: was the service unclear, was the timing wrong, was the request outside scope, or did the experience fail to explain a possible path? Patterns can improve service pages, content strategy, partnership referrals, and future offerings. Build a small taxonomy of outcomes and review it with the people responsible for marketing, sales, and delivery. The result is a lead system that learns without becoming invasive. When qualification is treated as a form of listening, the business receives better briefs, visitors receive better guidance, and human attention is reserved for the conversations where it can create the most value.` }
      ], sources: [source('FTC: Protecting Consumer Privacy and Data', 'https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/privacy-security'), source('NIST Privacy Framework', 'https://www.nist.gov/privacy-framework')]
    })
  });

  const additions = {
    'modern-concierge': [
      ['Write the welcome as a promise', `The opening message is where design intent becomes operational language. It should make three things immediately clear: what kind of help is available, what the system will do with a reply, and how a visitor can reach a person. “Tell me what you are trying to move forward” is stronger than a blank “How can I help?” when the business wants a project brief. Test the welcome with people who do not know the company. If they cannot explain the offer, the system is not ready. The words should carry the same restraint as the visual experience: attentive, capable, and free of pressure.`],
      ['Build a quiet service recovery', `A premium experience is defined partly by how it behaves at its edges. The concierge needs graceful responses for an unavailable answer, a broken booking link, a request outside scope, or a visitor who simply wants to speak to someone. Acknowledge the limit, preserve useful context, and offer one clear recovery route. Test those routes with the actual inbox, calendar, and client-services team. A thoughtful recovery script prevents small technical failures from becoming a signal that the brand is inattentive and gives the team a consistent way to learn from the exception.`],
      ['Set a cadence for editorial care', `Review the concierge like a high-traffic page or an important client-facing publication. Weekly, inspect unanswered questions, abandoned conversations, and routing anomalies. Monthly, confirm that service descriptions, examples, availability, and escalation contacts are current. Quarterly, revisit the charter and audience as the business changes. This cadence is deliberately modest: a small team can own it, and it establishes that the experience is living work. Answers remain current, transitions remain considered, and the digital welcome continues to reflect a business that is paying attention.`],
      ['From useful dialogue to durable relationship', `A first exchange should never try to close a complex engagement through cleverness. Its responsibility is to leave the visitor with more clarity than they arrived with and make the next move feel proportionate. Sometimes that is a scheduled conversation; sometimes it is a relevant case study, a concise checklist, or an honest referral. Measure whether the recommendation was useful even when it did not become immediate revenue. Over time, the organization learns which questions signal readiness, which pages create confusion, and where a human conversation creates the most value.`],
      ['Prototype the moment before production', `Before connecting a live CRM or calendar, role-play the dialogue with real examples. Give the concierge incomplete, contradictory, impatient, and highly specific requests. Ask a client-services colleague to play the visitor and a delivery lead to judge the resulting brief. This inexpensive rehearsal exposes whether the language is too sales-led, whether a question carries hidden assumptions, and whether the escalation instructions can actually be followed. Document the changes that result. The exercise is valuable precisely because it makes service standards tangible before automation has made the mistakes repeatable.`],
      ['Protect the visitor’s sense of pace', `Not every visitor wants a conversation at the same speed. Some are comparing options quietly; others are facing a deadline and need to know whether the business can help. Design for both without using urgency as pressure. Offer an easy route to browse, a concise route to describe a need, and a visible human contact path. Do not repeatedly interrupt a visitor who has declined assistance. Respecting pace is a form of digital etiquette. It keeps the interface from feeling like a trap and makes a voluntary next step more meaningful when it occurs.`]
    ],
    'responsible-ai-guide': [
      ['Keep a decision register', `Principles become accountable only when they are connected to decisions. Maintain a short register for consequential uses: purpose, owner, risk tier, data categories, model or vendor, evaluation date, approval conditions, and current status. This is not a bureaucratic archive for its own sake. It gives leaders a common record when someone asks why a system was permitted, what assumptions it relies on, or who is responsible for a change. It also makes renewal and retirement easier because the business is not reconstructing history from email threads.`],
      ['Procure for stewardship, not novelty', `Vendor review should test the conditions that will matter after the pilot: documentation quality, security controls, retention and deletion options, access management, data-use terms, service reliability, support, and the ability to export essential records. Ask how material changes are communicated and what happens when a capability is withdrawn. A compelling model demonstration cannot answer those questions. Procurement, legal, security, and operating teams should have a shared view of the use case before a contract makes a temporary experiment difficult to unwind.`],
      ['Make disclosure part of experience design', `When AI assists a customer-facing interaction, disclosure should appear where it helps a person make an informed choice—not be buried in a policy page. The language can be brief and calm: explain what the assistant can help with, that a person remains available, and how information will be handled. Pair disclosure with agency: a route to a human, a correction mechanism, and a way to decline unnecessary collection. Done well, this makes the organization look confident enough to be direct about the tools it uses and careful enough to preserve choice.`],
      ['Review the program as context changes', `Governance cannot be set once and forgotten because the business, the sources, and the technology all move. Establish a recurring review that considers new use cases, model or vendor changes, complaints, evaluation results, incidents, regulations relevant to the organization, and gaps in training. Invite the people who see the system at its edges: support teams, subject experts, and customers where appropriate. Their observations reveal the difference between a compliant-looking program and one that is actually operating safely. Record resulting decisions, owners, due dates, and the evidence required for closure.`],
      ['Budget for assurance', `Assurance work is not a drag on value; it is part of the cost of earning value safely. Budget for source curation, testing, accessibility review, training, support, incident handling, and periodic reassessment alongside licenses or development. When those activities are omitted from the business case, they do not disappear—someone performs them informally after a problem, or the service quietly degrades. A visible assurance budget lets leaders compare alternatives honestly and prevents risk controls from becoming unfunded aspirations. It also lets an executive team see the true trade between a faster launch and a more resilient service, instead of discovering that trade only after trust has been put at risk.`],
      ['Give employees a safe question channel', `People will encounter edge cases before a governance committee does. Provide a straightforward route for employees to ask whether a use is approved, report a confusing output, or flag a potential harm without fearing that curiosity will be punished. Answer patterns can become future guidance, training examples, or changes to the system. This creates early warning and reinforces that responsible use is collective practice, not a rulebook handed down by a distant function. The organization becomes more capable when uncertainty can be surfaced while it is still small. Make response ownership and expected turnaround explicit, then publish anonymized lessons from meaningful questions. Visible follow-through turns a reporting channel from a symbolic inbox into evidence that the organization values thoughtful judgment.`]
    ]
  };
  Object.entries(additions).forEach(([slug, sections]) => {
    if (window.AnchorNoteIssues[slug]) {
      window.AnchorNoteIssues[slug].sections.push(...sections.map(([heading, body]) => ({ heading, body })));
    }
  });

  const fieldEditions = {
    'pilots-that-learn': {
      operating: 'a pilot team that is testing a source-grounded assistant for account managers preparing client updates',
      evidence: 'a comparison between the existing preparation path and a sampled set of reviewed assistant-supported briefs, including factual corrections and time spent locating approved material',
      failure: 'the assistant producing a confident recommendation from an outdated source while the account manager assumes the answer is current',
      adoption: 'a weekly working session in which participating account managers bring one real case, review where the system helped or hindered, and nominate the next adjustment',
      investment: 'a six-week controlled trial with named source owners, a simple feedback channel, and a decision meeting scheduled before the test begins'
    },
    'buy-configure-build': {
      operating: 'a services business that needs to turn scattered discovery notes into a dependable project brief without changing the relationship-led character of its intake',
      evidence: 'the number of manual reconciliations removed, the completeness of the resulting brief, the time required for review, and the ease with which the business can change a rule or source',
      failure: 'a tailored interface that looks distinctive but silently depends on brittle integrations, undocumented prompts, and a vendor setting nobody owns',
      adoption: 'a cross-functional review where delivery, sales, technology, and finance test the same workflow against their practical needs before committing to a platform',
      investment: 'a staged architecture decision that funds discovery and configuration first, then authorizes custom work only when the evidence shows that it is strategically necessary'
    },
    'brand-voice-asset': {
      operating: 'a growing studio that wants every proposal, service page, newsletter, and AI-assisted response to feel like one considered point of view rather than a collection of individual styles',
      evidence: 'editor revision patterns, customer comprehension, source accuracy, the consistency of calls to action, and the time it takes a new team member to produce an approved draft',
      failure: 'an assistant repeating old positioning or polished but unsupported language because it was given a large archive with no current editorial authority',
      adoption: 'short editorial studios in which writers compare a real draft with the approved voice examples and record the specific decision behind each revision',
      investment: 'a maintained source library, a visible editorial owner, and a monthly review of language that has become stale, risky, or no longer true'
    },
    'education-program': {
      operating: 'a mixed team of marketers, project leaders, and client-services professionals who need to use approved AI tools without turning their daily work into an experiment',
      evidence: 'whether participants can complete a realistic role task more clearly and safely, the questions they escalate, the quality of review, and the use of the shared working library',
      failure: 'a well-attended workshop that leaves people with clever prompts but no practical sense of when to verify, when to stop, or where to ask for help',
      adoption: 'role-based practice sessions followed by office hours where participants bring unfinished work and learn from one another’s actual constraints',
      investment: 'a quarterly learning rhythm with a concise orientation, maintained examples, manager coaching, and an advanced group of internal champions'
    },
    'boardroom-value': {
      operating: 'an executive team deciding whether to support several AI initiatives that range from internal workflow relief to a new customer-facing capability',
      evidence: 'a balanced account of time, quality, risk, customer response, operating cost, and the organization’s ability to maintain the capability after the pilot team moves on',
      failure: 'a promising demonstration being scaled because it is visible, while source stewardship, change support, and incident ownership remain unfunded assumptions',
      adoption: 'a decision cadence in which operating leaders bring both metrics and sampled real work to discuss what changed, what did not, and what should happen next',
      investment: 'a portfolio approach that makes small reversible bets, protects funds for assurance, and sets different decision gates for near-term workflow improvements and longer-term strategic learning'
    },
    'lead-qualification': {
      operating: 'a prospective client arriving with an important but not yet fully formed request for digital, brand, or AI support',
      evidence: 'the quality of the resulting brief, appropriate routing, time to a useful human response, client attendance at next steps, and feedback from the delivery team about fit',
      failure: 'a scoring system treating incomplete information as low value and quietly excluding an unusual but potentially important opportunity',
      adoption: 'regular transcript review by marketing, sales, and delivery so the language and routing rules reflect the actual work the business wants to win and serve well',
      investment: 'a measured intake system with clear consent, limited data collection, accountable follow-up, and an explicit route to a person whenever automation is not the right answer'
    }
  };
  const makeFieldSections = ({ operating, evidence, failure, adoption, investment }) => [
    ['Make the operating hypothesis observable', `For ${operating}, a plan becomes credible when it identifies the exact moment of work being changed. Name the trigger, the source material, the person who uses the result, the decision it informs, and the exception that must still reach human judgment. That level of definition keeps teams from evaluating a broad technology promise instead of the actual experience. It also makes ownership practical: people can see what belongs to the system, what belongs to the operator, and where a customer or colleague should be able to question the outcome. A durable implementation starts with an observable operating hypothesis, not a category label.`],
    ['Choose evidence before the result arrives', `The evidence should be agreed while the team can still be surprised by it. For this work, examine ${evidence}. Pair the measures with a small set of real cases and invite the people closest to the process to explain what the numbers miss. A faster path may be less useful if it creates hidden correction work; a polished response may be weak if it cannot be traced to approved information. Clear evidence standards keep a project from being declared successful by enthusiasm alone and give leaders a fair basis for deciding whether to expand, revise, or stop.`],
    ['Design explicitly for the costly failure', `The failure worth planning for is ${failure}. Teams should write the response before launch: how the issue is detected, which record is reviewed, who communicates with affected people, who may pause the workflow, and what must change before use resumes. This is not pessimism. It is service design for the moment that reveals whether the organization values accuracy and dignity over appearances. When the route is clear, frontline colleagues are more likely to raise a concern early, and customers are more likely to receive an honest, timely recovery instead of a defensive explanation.`],
    ['Treat participation as part of the build', `Adoption should be designed as ${adoption}. This changes implementation from an announcement into a working relationship with the people who will carry the new practice. Ask participants to test awkward cases, compare outputs with their existing standard, and identify the moment at which they would no longer trust the system. Publish what changes as a result. People do not need to agree that every tool is exciting; they need evidence that their experience is being used to improve a system that affects their work. That is how confidence becomes earned rather than requested.`],
    ['Fund the stewardship that makes value durable', `The appropriate investment is ${investment}. Its value lies in the operating discipline around the technology: source care, evaluation, access control, training, maintenance, and governance. Include these responsibilities in the project brief and the financial case rather than leaving them to a generous individual after launch. A service remains credible when it can be revised, explained, and responsibly retired if conditions change. That is the standard for a capability designed to endure: it improves a real outcome today while preserving the organization’s ability to exercise judgment tomorrow.`],
    ['Leave a decision trail people can use', `As the work develops, keep a concise record of the choices that shape it: what the team set out to improve, which sources were approved, where the system is allowed to assist, what evidence changed a decision, and which questions remain open. For ${operating}, this trail should be readable by the person who inherits the work six months later, not only by the team that built it. It prevents an implementation from becoming folklore and makes careful revision possible when a policy, vendor, customer expectation, or business priority changes. The record should include both the successful decision and the limitation discovered along the way. This is not paperwork detached from delivery. It is the practical memory of a service that expects to be accountable. When leadership can see the reasoning, staff can operate with confidence, and customers encounter a more consistent experience, the organization has created more than a useful tool: it has created a trustworthy practice.`]
  ];
  Object.entries(fieldEditions).forEach(([slug, data]) => {
    if (window.AnchorNoteIssues[slug]) window.AnchorNoteIssues[slug].sections.push(...makeFieldSections(data).map(([heading, body]) => ({ heading, body })));
  });
})();
